3.1.m:
All kinds of food, both chewable and eatable, are exceedingly splendid;
yet through contact with it they turn into feces—how then can that body bring happiness?
Fragrant and cool water turns into urine through contact with it;
tell me now, O monkey: how could that lump [of flesh] be beautiful?
Indeed, garments that are exceedingly white and clean
become soiled with sweat through contact with it—how could it be beautiful?
Listen to the supreme truth from me, O Hanūmat, son of the Wind!
In this pit of saṃsāra, no happiness at all is to be found.
First a living creature meets with birth, and after that, childhood;
afterwards he attains youth, and then experiences old age;
thereafter he meets death, and then undergoes birth again.
It is solely through the power of ignorance that man experiences suffering;
upon the cessation of that ignorance, however, he attains supreme bliss.
The cessation of ignorance comes from knowledge alone, not from ritual action.
Knowledge is truly the supreme Brahman; knowledge is born of the statements of the Upaniṣads.

The best among recipients is one devoted to Vedic conduct;
even higher than him is the recipient who has no śūdra's food in his belly;
he whose regular practice consists continuously of the Vedas, the Purāṇa mantras,
the worship of Śiva, Viṣṇu, and others, and the observances of the classes and life-stages;
and who is poor and has a family—such a recipient is said to be the foremost.
What is given to such a recipient truly yields righteousness, pleasure, wealth, and liberation.
In a sacred place, a gift given to one who is not a true recipient is especially condemned;
otherwise, one will become a chameleon for ten births;
for three births he will be a donkey, and a frog for two births;
for one birth an outcaste (cāṇḍāla), and then he will become a śūdra;
and after that he is born in turn as a kṣatriya, a vaiśya, and a brahmin,
and there he will be poor and afflicted with many diseases.
Thus many kinds of faults arise from giving to an unworthy recipient;
therefore, by every effort, one should have gifts given to worthy recipients.
If such a recipient is not found, then, with a prior vow,
designating a single worthy recipient, one should pour water onto the ground.
Upon the death of the designated recipient, one should bestow it upon his son;
should his death occur as well, one should offer it to Mahādeva.
Therefore, one should never give to an unworthy recipient, especially at a sacred ford.
Śrī Sūta said:
Addressed in this manner by Vasiṣṭha, that Dilīpa, O best of brahmins,
from that time forward gave excellent gifts to worthy recipients.
Therefore, at this holy place, at the bridge, even here, O foremost of sages,
if a worthy recipient is found, then one should give wealth and the like.
If not, having made a vow, designating an eminent and worthy recipient,
one should pour water upon the earth with devotion.
Having returned to one's own village, one should afterwards offer it to that recipient.
Otherwise, the wealth previously resolved upon will cause a loss of merit.
He does not experience suffering again, but attains sāyujya liberation.
